WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.769
-Our next guest
is a talented actor

00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:04.838
you know from his work
in "Burn Notice," "Fargo,"

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:07.040
and his role as
Detective Frank Cosgrove

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.541
on the hit series "Law &amp; Order."

00:00:08.541 --> 00:00:11.678
The season finale airs Thursday
night at 8:00 here on NBC.

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:12.912
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:14.847
-Did you see anyone
in the immediate vicinity?

00:00:14.847 --> 00:00:16.181
-No.
-No one?

00:00:16.181 --> 00:00:18.450
You didn't see anyone running
or jumping into a car?

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.153
-No.
I'm sorry.

00:00:20.153 --> 00:00:21.954
I was so focused
on the man who got shot.

00:00:21.954 --> 00:00:24.323
-How far away were you when
you heard the two loud pops?

00:00:24.323 --> 00:00:26.926
-I don't know.
About 30 yards.

00:00:26.926 --> 00:00:30.429
-And you ran toward the sound
of the gunshots,

00:00:30.429 --> 00:00:32.431
which means you were there
five or six seconds

00:00:32.431 --> 00:00:34.167
after the shooting.
-What's your point?

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:36.769
-My point is, I find it hard to
believe you didn't see anybody.

00:00:36.769 --> 00:00:37.770
-Hey, come on, Frank.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:39.038
-I'm just trying
to motivate her.

00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:40.439
-Okay, okay.

00:00:40.439 --> 00:00:43.443
-Please welcome to the show
Jeffrey Donovan, everybody!

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:45.378
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:50.883 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:50.883 --> 00:00:56.388 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:56.388 --> 00:00:58.424
Welcome, Jeffrey!
So happy to have you here.

00:00:58.424 --> 00:00:59.959
-I am so nervous.

00:00:59.959 --> 00:01:02.795
-Yeah, well, I mean, I will say,
it's very rare for a first guest

00:01:02.795 --> 00:01:05.297
to plug the second guest
as much as Sarah did for you.

00:01:05.297 --> 00:01:07.133
-Oh, my God,
and I'm such a fan of hers.

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:09.102
I was watching, going,
"Sarah Silverman

00:01:09.102 --> 00:01:11.771
is doing an impression
of me doing my character."

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:13.839
-Yeah.
-It was very surreal.

00:01:13.839 --> 00:01:15.808
I was texting my wife.
I said, "I'm so nervous."

00:01:15.808 --> 00:01:17.844
She said, "Just go out there,
be yourself.

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:19.979
Just be relaxed and boring."

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:21.280
-That's so sweet.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:23.750
I feel like that's something
my wife would say.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:25.551
This is a very
New England-centric show.

00:01:25.551 --> 00:01:27.720
Sarah and I from the same
hometown in New Hampshire.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.889
What town in Massachusetts
are you from?

00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:31.090
-A little town called --

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.192
Well, to normal people,
you say "Amesbury,"

00:01:33.192 --> 00:01:34.560
but where I'm from "Amesbry."

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.629
-"Amesbry."
-Yeah, Amesbury, Massachusetts.

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:40.499
-Gotcha. I know your Twitter
handle is WickedpissaJD,

00:01:40.499 --> 00:01:43.202
which seems like you're
leaning in to being from the --

00:01:43.202 --> 00:01:45.238
-Yeah, yeah.
I mean, growing up, like,

00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:47.706
you know, I had
a wicked odd accent.

00:01:47.706 --> 00:01:50.143
I got rid of it 'cause of school
and acting and things like that.

00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:52.478
My brother still has it,
actually.

00:01:52.478 --> 00:01:57.417
He says something like this,
which I'd have to translate.

00:01:57.417 --> 00:01:59.819
"The party was really good.

00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:02.622
We ran out of beer, so we had to
go to the liquor store."

00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:03.856
So, he would say,

00:02:03.856 --> 00:02:06.425
"Uh, me and Mark got
wicked cocked last night.

00:02:06.425 --> 00:02:09.095
The pony went dry,
so we went on a packie run."

00:02:09.095 --> 00:02:10.830
-Oh, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.631
All that, that does
make sense to me.

00:02:12.631 --> 00:02:16.034
I'm like, "Oh,
my native tongue," yeah.

00:02:16.034 --> 00:02:18.604
So, your wife -- speaking
of your wife, very impressive.

00:02:18.604 --> 00:02:22.908
She ran the Boston Marathon, I
guess, just last month, right?

00:02:22.908 --> 00:02:25.545
Which, you know, it's a big deal
and not an easy --

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:28.114
I mean, no marathon is easy, but
that one's particularly hard.

00:02:28.114 --> 00:02:30.049
-Yeah, they say that's
one of the most difficult,

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:31.484
and it's the hardest
to get into.

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:32.952
You have to qualify
to get into it.

00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:34.353
You can't just show up
and run it

00:02:34.353 --> 00:02:36.556
like the New York City Marathon.
-Yeah.

00:02:36.556 --> 00:02:38.558
So, it's not a contest, Jeffrey.

00:02:38.558 --> 00:02:39.959
-It is.
Boston's better.

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:41.527
[ Laughter ]

00:02:41.527 --> 00:02:44.330
Yeah, she qualified and then
she ran her personal best.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:45.931
She ran in
3 hours and 17 minutes.

00:02:45.931 --> 00:02:48.301
-That is -- Your wife
knows how to run, man.

00:02:48.301 --> 00:02:49.769
-I'm very proud of her.

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:52.105
-That is a no-foolin'
marathon time.

00:02:52.105 --> 00:02:54.239
-Then, I thought, "Oh, well,
she did something great.

00:02:54.239 --> 00:02:56.676
I'm gonna treat myself," which,
you know, that's how it works.

00:02:56.676 --> 00:02:59.678
So, I don't get back
to Boston much.

00:02:59.678 --> 00:03:02.348
So, I grew up on steamers,
steamed clams.

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:04.917
-Yeah.
-We say "steam-uhs."

00:03:04.917 --> 00:03:06.352
And so I called a restaurant.

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:08.754
I was like, "Hey, yeah, I want
to order some 'steam-uhs.'"

00:03:08.754 --> 00:03:11.958
They said, "What?"
"Steam-uhs."

00:03:11.958 --> 00:03:13.993
Have I lost my Boston accent?

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:16.995
"Steam-uhs, steam-uhs,
steam-uhs."

00:03:16.995 --> 00:03:19.264
They're like, "Sorry, we don't
know what you're saying."

00:03:19.264 --> 00:03:20.366
"They're clams.

00:03:20.366 --> 00:03:22.067
You put them in a pot,
you boil them."

00:03:22.067 --> 00:03:23.903
"You mean mussels?"

00:03:23.903 --> 00:03:25.138
"No, no."
-Oh, my God.

00:03:25.138 --> 00:03:26.939
Did you go burn that place down?

00:03:26.939 --> 00:03:28.274
[ Laughter ]

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:29.976
-I called like five restaurants.

00:03:29.976 --> 00:03:31.376
No one knew what
"steam-uhs" were.

00:03:31.376 --> 00:03:33.045
-Oh, Boston's changed.

00:03:33.045 --> 00:03:35.748
Now, you know, I will say,
you did deserve a treat,

00:03:35.748 --> 00:03:38.884
because, you know, obviously,
your wife is a very fast woman

00:03:38.884 --> 00:03:41.720
and she was running, but
that's 3 hours and 17 minutes

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:43.456
where you were in charge
of four children,

00:03:43.456 --> 00:03:44.523
because you have four kids.

00:03:44.523 --> 00:03:46.158
-Much harder.
-Much harder, yeah.

00:03:46.158 --> 00:03:48.527
-Much harder.
But they were great.

00:03:48.527 --> 00:03:50.929
I kept on telling them,
"We're gonna go watch Mom."

00:03:50.929 --> 00:03:52.932
And we got down there,
I had a nice pass.

00:03:52.932 --> 00:03:54.000
NBC was very nice.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.801
They, like, let me be
in the bleachers.

00:03:55.801 --> 00:03:58.104
And I was sitting there
with the four kids,

00:03:58.104 --> 00:03:59.738
and there she came running down.

00:03:59.738 --> 00:04:01.574
You see these other guys.

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:03.209
You see guys like this,
running like this,

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:05.244
going, "Oh, my God, oh, my God."

00:04:05.244 --> 00:04:07.714
See my wife going...
-Oh, yeah.

00:04:07.714 --> 00:04:10.349
-She just runs by like it was --

00:04:10.349 --> 00:04:13.052
And then the youngest
is a year and a half,

00:04:13.052 --> 00:04:16.356
and he's still on mommy milk,
so she went right back to work.

00:04:16.356 --> 00:04:18.758
-She deserves two medals
for that, yeah.

00:04:18.758 --> 00:04:19.859
Hey, so, I have to ask,

00:04:19.859 --> 00:04:21.927
you know, again,
we were talking backstage.

00:04:21.927 --> 00:04:24.263
We both have loved this
franchise for a long time,

00:04:24.263 --> 00:04:26.665
but you've actually appeared
two other times

00:04:26.665 --> 00:04:30.002
as two different characters
on "Law &amp; Order" over the years.

00:04:30.002 --> 00:04:31.871
So you have quite
a nice trajectory.

00:04:31.871 --> 00:04:33.205
-Yeah, I got out of school,

00:04:33.205 --> 00:04:35.374
and everyone wanted
to be on "Law &amp; Order."

00:04:35.374 --> 00:04:37.309
It was a badge of honor.

00:04:37.309 --> 00:04:39.912
And so I started off
as a dead guy.

00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:41.413
-Oh, congrats!
-Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:04:41.413 --> 00:04:43.616
-So, like, never moved?
Never moved a muscle?

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:45.951
-No, yeah, I was dead,
but then you had a flashback

00:04:45.951 --> 00:04:47.720
on a videotape,
so I had a few lines.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.054
-Okay, good.

00:04:49.054 --> 00:04:52.258
-And then like 10 years later,
I came back as a suspect.

00:04:52.258 --> 00:04:53.993
-Uh-huh.
-Killed someone.

00:04:53.993 --> 00:04:55.561
-Oh!
So not just a suspect.

00:04:55.561 --> 00:04:57.129
-I was a murderer.
-Yeah, okay, great.

00:04:57.129 --> 00:04:59.164
-But it was for a good reason.
-Oh, interesting.

00:04:59.164 --> 00:05:01.534
I'll have to go back
and watch that one.

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:04.069
-I was a dad protecting
my daughter's --

00:05:04.069 --> 00:05:05.370
What's that, her mortal --

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:07.006
-Tell it to the jury.

00:05:07.006 --> 00:05:08.841
[ Laughter ]

00:05:08.841 --> 00:05:12.678
-And then, yeah,
Mr. Wolf called and said,

00:05:12.678 --> 00:05:14.847
"You want to play
Detective Cosgrove?"

00:05:14.847 --> 00:05:16.882
And I dropped the phone,
picked it back up,

00:05:16.882 --> 00:05:19.352
and I'm so, so grateful.

00:05:19.352 --> 00:05:21.353
-Even though
you threw a little shade

00:05:21.353 --> 00:05:22.622
at the New York City Marathon.

00:05:22.622 --> 00:05:24.323
I imagine one of the great
things about the show,

00:05:24.323 --> 00:05:25.991
and I've asked Anthony
about this, as well,

00:05:25.991 --> 00:05:27.160
but being out in the streets --

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:28.560
You shoot this show
in the streets.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:29.829
Like, people, it must be great,

00:05:29.829 --> 00:05:31.798
New Yorkers getting to watch you
do a show like this.

00:05:31.798 --> 00:05:33.632
It's been a franchise
in the city for so long.

00:05:33.632 --> 00:05:36.568
-Yeah, it's been --
The city's been so welcoming.

00:05:36.568 --> 00:05:37.803
But it's an amazing thing

00:05:37.803 --> 00:05:39.972
to walk down the street
with Anthony Anderson.

00:05:39.972 --> 00:05:44.577
I mean, I'm vaguely familiar,
basically, my whole life.

00:05:44.577 --> 00:05:45.911
People usually think that, like,

00:05:45.911 --> 00:05:47.413
they went to high school
with me.

00:05:47.413 --> 00:05:49.648
-Right.
-"Did we -- Fourth grade?"

00:05:49.648 --> 00:05:53.319
No, but Anthony is like
the mayor of the city.

00:05:53.319 --> 00:05:56.088
And we walk down, shooting,
in the middle of the take,

00:05:56.088 --> 00:05:59.024
they'll yell,
"Anthony, Anthony!"

00:05:59.024 --> 00:06:00.726
He won't break stride.

00:06:00.726 --> 00:06:04.196
He'll just be like,
"Hey, how you doing?!

00:06:04.196 --> 00:06:05.697
What's goin' on?!

00:06:05.697 --> 00:06:07.433
I'm shootin' a show!"

00:06:07.433 --> 00:06:09.134
[ Laughter ]

00:06:09.134 --> 00:06:10.403
-There you go.

00:06:10.403 --> 00:06:12.070
And people are gonna listen
to Anthony Anderson.

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:14.073
-And what do you got?

00:06:14.073 --> 00:06:15.708
-Hey, man,
thanks for being here.

00:06:15.708 --> 00:06:17.477
Congrats on the show.
It's wonderful.

00:06:17.477 --> 00:06:19.078
Jeffrey Donovan, everybody.

00:06:19.078 --> 00:06:20.646
Season finale of "Law &amp; Order"

00:06:20.646 --> 00:06:22.714
airs tomorrow night at 8:00
here on NBC.

00:06:22.714 --> 00:06:24.384
We'll be right back
with Sleaford Mods.

